when we talk about the future of work  and we talk about  what would happen if we don't move  on diversity equity and inclusion i  don't  honestly think that there is a future  that that's possible studies have shown  that more diverse teams tend to perform  better  and many companies have made commitments  to hiring a more diverse workforce  yet creating inclusive work cultures  that are truly equitable and free of  bias  remains a formidable challenge for  companies especially since some of the  biggest obstacles  are the very employees they are trying  to change  one of the challenges uh in this space  is that people do have really strong  feelings about what they  should do and what's right but the  strategies that organizations have used  pretty much universally  are not effective they're ineffective  and sometimes make things worse  so many of us have incredible intentions  but terrible actions  and unless you can get your intention on  your action to align  then your intention is in vain but there  may be actions that companies and their  employees can take  that can mean change is finally starting  to happen  if business would step in and do this  work  i think it would change society outside  of the walls of business  and yes it is disruptive technology was  disruptive  think about the companies that said we  don't really want to touch technology  they're not here anymore  but from the disruption comes change  so there's a very strong link between  companies who  are strong at diversity equity and  inclusion and  increased organizational performance  this is dr david rock he's the ceo and  co-founder of the neuro leadership  institute  a consultancy company that advises  organizations on management strategies  based on science and evidence-based  practices  the most kind of salient way to  understand this is that  we all see the world very very  differently and  when you get a homogeneous team or a  team that's all basically the same  they actually make more cognitive errors  they don't see errors that other people  have made  and they're far less creative and far  less innovative than  a diverse team while these diverse  groups may be more efficient  creative and faster at finding and  solving problems  they often feel like they are being less  efficient and less confident in their  work  partly due to a phenomenon called  cognitive elaboration which is a long  word for  saying you're going to think harder the  teams are thinking harder  thinking is generally aversive and so we  we feel more confident and comfortable  in teams that are  more homogeneous where people basically  agree with us all the time  and it turns out that feels good but the  team is nowhere near as successful  if companies want to see the positive  effects of a diverse and inclusive  workforce  they need to find ways to mitigate that  naturally occurring friction  essentially they need to have their  employees be comfortable  being uncomfortable in business we go to  work we do our jobs and we leave  and we are so weary  of pushing against the grain with a  colleague with a co-worker with an  employer et cetera with an employee  and i think if we truly want to grow and  get to know each other better  we do have to embrace some of that  uncomfortable dialogue for the sake of  growth  this is former nfl linebacker and fox  sports analyst emmanuel acho  and in 2020 he launched a youtube series  all about getting  uncomfortable welcome to another episode  of uncomfortable conversations with a  black man  when's the last time you sat down just  to have a conversation  uh with a group of black people garrett  i can be honest with you i don't know  that i've ever had a conversation with a  group of black people  i created uncomfortable conversations  with a black man out of an overflow of  my heart  after the murder of george floyd i  realized i had to do something  the series would go on to amass over 17  million views  inspire two books and most recently  emmanuel launched a podcast with  accenture  north america ceo jimmy etheridge  so many ceos executives at companies  they want to serve  those under them or those with them but  they think they're doing what's best  but unless you ask them what is best you  will be sitting here  spitting your wheels and actually not  serving the person at all  primary example um jimmy and i recently  spoke to  a woman who was hearing disabled and we  started that conversation on that  episode  um i used words like um that she was  different  you know modified and all these other  fanciful words and but the first  question i asked  hey i'm going to start with this  disabled  is that wrong to say or not and she  actually told me she cringed when i said  different she said because she's not  different she is disabled and  and like she doesn't find any weakness  in that but she actually finds strength  in that  if i don't start with what is a very  uncomfortable question  i'm sitting here thinking i'm being  polite to try to  you know be kind and in all honesty  she's cringing on the inside  and that's what so many of us do in the  workplace well this is what i'm going to  do to make sure i help these black men  out  this is what i'm going to do to help out  these black women and so my advice is  talk to the people in whom you are  trying to serve  talk to the people in whom you are  trying to honor i had great intentions  my intentions were amazing by trying to  use a word that i perceived to be more  kind and disabled  my actions terrible in the last decade  or so  more businesses have promised to make  diversity a major priority  tech giants like apple google and  facebook  have committed to sharing their internal  diversity numbers and annual reports  and while there's been an increase in  the overall percentage of employees of  color at these companies  for some groups only minimal progress  has been made  when it comes to black or latinx  employees their percentage in the last  six years has only been raised one to  three percent  while hiring is only step one retaining  and growing diversity is a major focus  as well  and something that companies have also  struggled with just having bodies in the  building  doesn't get you anywhere you have to be  able to leverage that diversity  and that means you need systems you need  metrics you need accountability  this is janet stovall a veteran of the  corporate communications world and a  senior client  strategist for the neuro leadership  institute at nli we talk about  priorities  habits and systems so the first thing  you have to do  is you have to make this a priority and  it has to be a priority like any other  business priority  that a company decides that it wants to  pursue  then you have to look at the habits that  are in place  to make people either live up to those  priorities or not  and so once you prioritize you look at  the habits that are getting in the way  of that priority and then you build a  system to make sure that you can change  those habits  but according to nli research some of  the most common strategies that  companies turn to  in order to create inclusion can often  have the opposite effect  one example is in the area of  unconscious bias training  it feels really intuitive to mandate  you know like bias education you know  bias is clearly a problem  it seems perfectly logical to say well  look everyone should really go through  bias training  there's very good evidence that that  does nothing or makes things worse i  mean  you might get a program that gets small  amount of results but on  average requiring people to do  awareness-building bias training is not  going to help very much in 2016  researchers who study 30 years of data  from over 800 companies  wrote in the harvard business review  that the positive effects of diversity  training rarely last beyond a day or two  and a number of studies suggest that it  can activate bias  or spark a backlash now bias is  everywhere because  at the end of the day everybody is born  with bias  the problem is when bias gets into  business decisions  we sometimes make sub-optimal decisions  and  we we actually can't see the vast  majority of our own biases  no matter how much we want to or how  much we know about them but what we  realized is you could do something about  bias in  teams their strategy takes over 150  forms of bias  and groups them into five broad  categories  then instead of asking individuals to  self-police their own actions  companies can create systems to  encourage teams to get into the habit of  identifying biases as they happen  what we found is when you teach that to  people relatively quickly  people start to see these biases as they  happen  and relatively quickly they're able to  label bias in a process or in a decision  and then work out what to do to address  it and our studies are ongoing of course  in this space but we're seeing  about 78 of people who learn this  framework start to use it weekly at  least one to three times a week  it doesn't get us anywhere pointing the  finger  what we have to be able to do instead of  giving people labels is we have to be  able to label behaviors  if we can label behaviors and then agree  on a common language for those behaviors  we can talk about what people do  separate from what they  are so labeling people racist doesn't  help  in the business workplace it just  doesn't help at all but coming up with  things that help you  change the behavior that might be racist  behavior  that's what changes things  one of the most important things to do  with each and every individual  is is what we call find common ground  and  this is a habit of of creating in-group  with people  and and really bringing each of you into  the same  group in a sense um or in the brain it's  it's both of you being in this circle  that we all  draw around ourselves and being on that  same team  and so the habit is in every interaction  whether you're an employee or  a manager or leader in every interaction  you're really finding  common ground with that person ideally  creating  shared goals and this creates a really  strong sense of inclusion  i heard it said one time you know i  can't change hearts but i can change  laws  well i'd like to paraphrase that and say  you know i can't change what you  think but i can certainly change what  you do and as long as i change what you  do  i'll end up getting the results that i  want improving workplace diversity and  inclusion  isn't just about building one set of  habits or eliminating a certain behavior  and it is likely to be uncomfortable but  if the future of work can truly leverage  diverse and inclusive cultures  it can have effects beyond just the  company's bottom line  i truly believe that the business  workplace  is the only place that some people ever  come into contact with anybody who's  different from them  we can spend our lives around people who  are no different from us  outside of work all we want but if we go  to work  and if you have an organization that  decides it is committed  to diversity equity and inclusion and it  is committed enough  that it is going to have priorities  habits and systems  in place to make that grow and be  what it needs to be you have a choice if  you work in that environment you're  either going to get on with it or you're  not  and so i think the business is the one  entity  that could actually change this because  no place else  has that reach no place else has that  context  no place else has the power the money  and the resources  to change things than the commercial  world and so if we step into this and do  this work i think it'll change society

